    "## ⚡ 2. Why Activation Functions Matter\n",
    "\n",
    "Without an activation function, a stack of linear transformations remains linear. Adding a non-linear activation allows a network to learn complex, curved patterns. This is the key reason activations make deep networks more expressive than a purely linear stack."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6ff5ae63",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 📈 3. Common Activation Functions\n",
    "\n",
    "| Function | Output range | Typical use |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **ReLU** | 0 to +∞ | Hidden layers — default choice |\n",
    "| **Sigmoid** | 0 to 1 | Binary-classification output |\n",
    "| **Softmax** | 0 to 1, sums to 1 | Multi-class output |\n",
    "| **Tanh** | -1 to +1 | Hidden layers when zero-centered output helps |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Practical rule:** ReLU for hidden layers by default; sigmoid for binary output; softmax for multi-class output; no activation (linear) for regression."
   ]
  },
